course: dictionary
lesson: 4954
title: Small word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «In» is one of the most common words in English, and it can do lots of different jobs. Most of the time it tells you where something is, when something happens, or how something is done. Context tells you which job it is doing.
explain: «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs.» Here in shows that the animal is inside something, caught in a ring. «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds» uses in to show a time: it takes only a few seconds.
explain: In can also be a small word that means inside or at home, as in «Come in!». Sometimes casual talk changes the meaning: «I'm in» means count me in, I will join. Use the sentence around in to decide which meaning it has.
fact: In shows that something is inside something else, as in «in the box».
fact: In shows a time, as in «in June», «in the morning» or «in five minutes».
fact: In shows a way or a state, as in «in pairs», «in red» or «in a hurry».
fact: In shows a part of a whole, as in «one in ten».
fact: In the exam voice, «in excess of» means more than, so «in excess of £5» means more than £5.
exercise: «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs.» What does in show here? | A time, like in June | *Something inside something else | A part of a whole, like one in ten | Fashionable
exercise: «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds.» What does in show here? | A way of doing something | Inside a box | *A time | Fashionable
exercise: Mum says, «Come in, your tea is ready!» What does in mean? | *Inside, at home | Fashionable | Part of a whole | Go outside to play
test: «One in ten children in our class wears glasses.» What does the first in show? | Inside a box | A time | A way or a state | *A part of a whole
test: Ella says, «Count me in for the trip!» What does in mean here? | Fashionable hats | Inside a box | *Join in, I will be part of it | A time, like in June
exam: «The bill came to a cost in excess of £5.» What does in excess of mean? | Less than £5 | Inside a box | *More than £5 | Exactly £5
